<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="dark">
  <head>
    <title><%= content_for(:title) || "Chopshop Studio" %> · Chopshop</title>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">
    <%= csrf_meta_tags %>
    <%= csp_meta_tag %>
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag "application", "palette-tool", "data-turbo-track": "reload" %>
    <%= javascript_importmap_tags(current_user ? "application" : "login") %>
  </head>

  <body>
    <% if current_user %>
      <nav class="topbar">
        <div class="container nav-content">
          <a href="<%= root_path %>" class="logo">
            <div class="logo-icon">✂</div>
            <div class="logo-text">Chopshop<span>Studio</span></div>
          </a>
          <ul class="nav-links">
            <li><a href="<%= root_path %>">Jobs</a></li>
            <li><a href="<%= new_job_path %>">Run Job</a></li>
            <li><a href="<%= json_path %>">JSON</a></li>
            <li><a href="<%= flux_path %>">FLUX</a></li>
            <li><a href="<%= jev_path %>">JEV</a></li>
            <%# ONE entry for ONE service: BP is Blend-Plot, the two halves -- plot
                (documents) and blend (3D) -- on one page. It replaced the separate
                PLOT and BLENDER links; /plot and /blender redirect here so nothing
                that already pointed at them 404s. %>
            <li><a href="<%= bp_path %>">BP</a></li>
            <%# TOURNAMENT and DATASETS were the last two entries here. Both are
                REFERENCE surfaces — the same reason STATUS left the header row
                (see status_page_test), and the row does not grow for a page that
                cannot act on a job. Each went to the surface that already owns
                its subject rather than to a menu of its own:
                  * TOURNAMENT is a footer button, beside the About one, because
                    it is a thing to look at and decides nothing.
                  * DATASETS is a POPUP on FLUX, because a prompt is taken FROM
                    the library and generated WITH flux — one subject, one page.
                The nav is the working path: Jobs · Run Job · JSON · FLUX · JEV ·
                BP, oldest to newest. %>
          </ul>
          <div class="nav-side">
            <% health = Job.runners.keys.map { |r| [r, RunnerClient.health(Job.runners[r])] } %>
            <details class="runner-menu">
              <summary class="runner-pill"><span class="dot"></span>runners ▾</summary>
              <div class="runner-dropdown">
                <% health.each do |name, h| %>
                  <div class="runner-row">
                    <span class="dot <%= h && h[:ok] ? 'dot-ok' : 'dot-down' %>"></span>
                    <span class="mono"><%= name %></span>
                    <span class="runner-status"><%= h && h[:ok] ? "up" : "down" %></span>
                  </div>
                <% end %>
              </div>
            </details>
            <%= button_to "Log out", logout_path, method: :delete, class: "btn btn-secondary btn-sm" %>
          </div>
        </div>
      </nav>

    <%# Scrapbook: a left sidebar drawer of favourited sources/inverts/candidates/
        proofs. It is its own store (storage/scrapbook), so it survives deleting
        the jobs its contents came from. Rendered on every authed page; the toggle
        is the fixed handle on the left edge. %>
    <%# The favourite button, defined ONCE for the whole app: this is the same
        partial the tiles and the rows render, cloned into the preview popup's
        toolbox by aether.js (csFavButtonNode). Rendering it here instead of
        writing a second button in JavaScript is what keeps the star, its
        data-* contract and the one delegated click handler identical wherever
        a favourite appears. The template's own job/name are filled in by the
        popup, so it ships empty. %>
    <template id="csFavTemplate">
      <%= render "scrapbook/fav_button", job_id: "", name: "", label: "this file", word: "Favourite" %>
    </template>
    <button type="button" class="scrapbook-toggle" id="scrapbookToggle"
            title="Scrapbook" aria-label="Open Scrapbook" aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="scrapbookSidebar">
      <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><polygon points="12 2 15.09 8.26 22 9.27 17 14.14 18.18 21.02 12 17.77 5.82 21.02 7 14.14 2 9.27 8.91 8.26 12 2"></polygon></svg>
    </button>
    <aside class="scrapbook-sidebar" id="scrapbookSidebar" aria-hidden="true" aria-label="Scrapbook">
      <header class="scrapbook-head">
        <h2 class="scrapbook-title">Scrapbook</h2>
      </header>
      <%# The close rides the drawer's OUTER edge, vertically centred: that edge
          is where the drawer meets the page, so the control sits on the
          boundary it closes rather than up in the header. An inline SVG ✕, not
          a word and not a glyph — ✕ is not in the pixel face, so it silently
          fell back at a different weight beside the title, and every other
          close in the app is an icon. The ring behind it is the app's orb
          accent, so it reads as the drawer's handle.
          This is the DRAWER's close, not a POPUP's: the popup standard is the
          × glyph in .popup-head, pinned by popup_close_standard_test.rb, and
          the two conventions stay separate. %>
      <button type="button" class="scrapbook-close" data-scrap-close
              title="Close Scrapbook" aria-label="Close Scrapbook">
        <span class="scrapbook-close-orb" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <%# The full accent effect, reused from the login scissor: the sunspot
            layers (.login-sun-*) are self-contained — their turn rates and
            balance are declared on the classes and the keyframes are global —
            so dropping them into this round button gives the drawer's close
            the same living light (drifting mottle, an intermittent burst, the
            slow pink section) with no second copy of the recipe. %>
        <span class="login-sun-layer" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="login-sun-burst" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="login-sun-pink" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"></line><line x1="6" y1="6" x2="18" y2="18"></line></svg>
      </button>
      <div class="scrapbook-body" id="scrapbookBody">
        <p class="scrapbook-empty">Nothing favourited yet.</p>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <%# Composite sidebar: the frame's SECOND panel, a vertical partner to the
        scrapbook drawer.
        #frame-vertical-stack — same left column, bottom-origin, and it becomes
        a two-band column when both panels are up (body.frame-split).
        Not a second store: it is the NAVIGATION frame over the composites
        table, filled from /composite/list, and every row opens the builder at
        /composite?id=N. Its own close orb carries the frame's cycle control,
        because in the composite-only state this panel is the only one on
        screen.
    #frame-vertical-stack %>
    <aside class="scrapbook-sidebar composite-sidebar" id="compositeSidebar" aria-hidden="true" aria-label="Composites">
      <header class="scrapbook-head">
        <h2 class="scrapbook-title">Composites</h2>
      </header>
      <button type="button" class="scrapbook-close" data-scrap-close
              title="Cycle side panels (double-click to close)" aria-label="Cycle side panels">
        <span class="scrapbook-close-orb" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="login-sun-layer" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="login-sun-burst" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="login-sun-pink" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"></line><line x1="6" y1="6" x2="18" y2="18"></line></svg>
      </button>
      <div class="scrapbook-body" id="compositeBody">
        <p class="scrapbook-empty">No composites yet.</p>
      </div>
    </aside>
    <% end %>

    <main class="container">
      <% if notice %><div class="flash notice"><%= notice %></div><% end %>
      <% if alert %><div class="flash alert"><%= alert %></div><% end %>
      <%= yield %>
    </main>

    <footer class="footer-note">
      <div class="container footer-inner">
        <span>chopshop-svg · trace → you pick → proof · the pipeline never picks a winner</span>
        <%# The bar's right-hand chrome: the About link with the check-engine
            light beside it. Wrapped so the two travel TOGETHER at the right
            edge -- .footer-inner spreads its children with space-between, so a
            bare third child would have parked the light in the middle of the
            bar, away from the link it belongs with.
            #engine-light-link — the footer's route to /status. %>
        <div class="footer-chrome">
          <%# The footer's reference buttons: the same pill footprint as a page
              action, so a reference surface reads as chrome rather than as a
              caption. TOURNAMENT joined the About button here when it left the
              sticky header; the two are the footer's whole link row and the
              check-engine light sits beside them. %>
          <a href="<%= tournament_path %>" class="footer-demo">Tournament</a>
          <%# The About page's button. The label is whitespace-padded into a
              LARGE button by .footer-demo's own padding (real whitespace inside
              the pill) rather than by spaces in the label, which HTML collapses. %>
          <a href="<%= about_path %>" class="footer-demo">???</a>
          <% if current_user %>
            <%# The check-engine light: a round orb carrying no word at all, just
                an engine glyph and a status dot, and it LINKS to the STATUS page
                rather than opening anything -- the telemetry and the ML/ranking
                configuration behind it are that page's own panels.
                It is the only way to STATUS: the link left the sticky header,
                which is also what keeps the bar's link row at six entries
                rather than growing a seventh.
                Gated on current_user because the BAR renders on every page,
                /login included, and the page it leads to reports the signed-in
                owner's own signal. %>
            <a class="engine-light <%= engine_light_cold? ? 'is-cold' : 'is-live' %>"
               href="<%= status_path %>"
               title="Engine / telemetry — <%= engine_light_state %>"
               aria-label="Engine / telemetry — <%= engine_light_state %>">
              <svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M14.7 6.3a1 1 0 0 0 0 1.4l1.6 1.6a1 1 0 0 0 1.4 0l3.77-3.77a6 6 0 0 1-7.94 7.94l-6.91 6.91a2.12 2.12 0 0 1-3-3l6.91-6.91a6 6 0 0 1 7.94-7.94l-3.76 3.76z"></path></svg>
              <span class="engine-light-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </a>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      </div>
    </footer>

    <% if flash[:welcome] %>
      <div class="welcome-overlay" id="welcomeOverlay" role="dialog" aria-label="Welcome">
        <div class="welcome-type" id="welcomeType" aria-live="polite"></div>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# Frame cycle: the close orb cycles the frame's states.
        #frame-cycle-orb — the state machine lives in app/javascript/aether.js
        (frameApply / frameCycle), NOT here: an inline script cannot survive a
        Turbo body swap, and it cannot share `frameState` with the drawer's own
        open/close. The order is star -> scrap -> both -> comp -> none, chosen so
        every state is reachable from a control that is still on screen in it. %>
  </body>
</html>
